   130	
   131	The first mutation slice adds Approve/Deny only when this exact Surface owns the
   132	turn's decision token. The public permission snapshot never exposes that token,
   133	and the Island must not weaken the daemon's authorization boundary.
   134	
   135	An expanded permission card is actionable only when all are true:
   136	
   137	- its `permission_id` exists in the focused Surface's live
   138	  `pending_permissions` signal;
   139	- the permission belongs to the currently focused session;
   140	- `active_decision_token` is present for that submitted turn.
   141	
   142	When actionable, the disclosure shows one compact action row: danger-ghost
   143	`Deny` followed by primary `Approve`. Both reuse `Daemon::decide_permission`,
   144	disable while deciding, and never add raw arguments to the Island. The redundant
   145	`Open session` action is hidden because the owning session is already focused.
   146	A background or foreign-surface permission stays read-only and offers only
   147	`Open session`; it must never render a button that will predictably fail token
   148	verification.
   149	
   150	`Allow for session`, inline reply, and dismiss/acknowledge remain deferred. This
   151	slice changes no daemon endpoint or token semantics.
   152	
   153	## Phase 3B — explicit stop control
   154	
   155	Queued/running request cards may expose one danger-ghost `Stop` action backed by
   156	the daemon's existing `POST /v1/requests/{request_id}/cancel` authority. The
   157	Surface tracks only whether that POST is in flight so double submission is
   158	impossible; it does not optimistically invent a cancelling request state. After
   159	a successful POST it refreshes the authoritative snapshots. The button reads
   160	`Stopping…` and disables while the request is in flight.
   161	
   162	A background running card may show `Open session` plus `Stop`. A focused running
   163	card hides redundant `Open session` and shows only `Stop`. Cancelling cards are
   164	read-only and show the daemon-owned stopping state. If a cancel fails, the
   165	existing concise status surface reports the error and the control becomes
   166	retryable.
   167	
   168	Inline reply/resume remains deferred because it needs explicit background-turn
   169	submission semantics; switching focus remains the only safe prompt path.
   170	
   171	## Future typed cards — CI, diffs, and todos
   172	
   173	CI/GitHub and todo disclosures follow the same plain-row grammar, but require
   174	new daemon-owned typed payloads rather than parsing logs or inventing local
   175	state.
